<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cipriano Castro</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1858–1924</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cipriano Castro was a Venezuelan military leader from the Andean state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tachira">Táchira</a> who seized power in 1899 and ruled as president until 1908. His refusal to pay foreign debts led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> Venezuelan ports in 1902–1903; while he was abroad for medical treatment in 1908, his vice-president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-vicente-gomez">Juan Vicente Gómez</a> took power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cipriano-castro.jpg" alt="Cipriano Castro" width="320" height="386" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicola Perscheid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cipriano_Castro_1908.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
